media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: put the capture's input converter back after a republished descriptor

Since 0117 a resolution change republishes the VidDec record, and the
picture that comes back is green. Measured in the ring, not on the wall:
grey areas read Cb ~ Cr ~ Y (taskbar 40/43/40, white 225/226/225) where a
clean cold start reads Cb ~ Cr ~ 128. The capture writes the HDMI
receiver's RGB into the NV16 ring without converting it, and the plane
reads it as YCbCr.

The switch that does it is the capture's input converter, MP_ICSC_VINCAP,
INCAP 0x06940824 bit 31 (1 = bypass). The firmware picks its state from
the colour format of the signal that caused the last signal event
(TFD attribute Signal_Format -> colorspace_config_mp: YUV -> bypass, RGB ->
the signal's colour space). Our record says yuv420_888, because that is
the only value that keeps the AFBD reader on NV16 -- so every republish
ends in bypass. The HDMI receiver's own re-lock sets BT.709 90 ms earlier,
and loses. The first publication does not show it only because it ends in
a source switch: the re-lock that follows is then the last word.
(Firmware side: doku/nachtlog/S9; device: S11, elog level 5.)

Ruled out: the record's colour format. rgb_888 there does give RGB_888 and
BT.709, but it also moves NRWinNode_ColorFormatConvert to AFBD format 4,
the chroma stride to 1280 and the picture to a much stronger green --
measured, and a dead end already listed in re/notes/DEAD-ENDS.md.

What works is THal_Vp_SetVideoRange with a changed value: the firmware
then evaluates its picture path for the VideoDec record (bypass) and once
more for the HDMI signal it still has cached (RGB -> BT.709), and the last
one writes the register. Full range and back to auto is two such changes
and leaves the range where the init sequence put it. No window rebuild,
no capture enable, no state machine; measured at 1080p and 720p, scaler
0x3200AAAA and strides untouched, 0x824 back to 0x0000000B, ring back to
YCbCr, taskbar grey on the wall.

Order matters, because Marco saw the green picture first and the fix a
moment later. The publication's rebuild ends by switching the capture
off; wait for that, restore the converter while the capture is off (the
ring keeps the previous picture, no green frame is ever written), then
enable. Checked once more after the enable holds and after the first
publication's source switch -- both should be no-ops and say so in the
log if they are not.

--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-08 00:57:18.850604646 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-08 00:58:15.841710976 +0200
@@ -195,6 +195,17 @@
 #define H713_INCAP_ENABLE_BIT	BIT(31)
 
 /*
+ * The capture's input colour converter, MP_ICSC_VINCAP in the firmware's own
+ * terms. Bit 31 set is "bypass": the HDMI receiver's RGB goes into the NV16
+ * ring unconverted, the plane reads it as YCbCr and the picture is green.
+ * Read here only -- the firmware is asked to rewrite it, see
+ * h713_hdmirx_restore_icsc(). Polarity from the firmware's own module table
+ * (doku/nachtlog/S9 section 4), confirmed on the device (S11).
+ */
+#define H713_INCAP_ICSC		0x824
+#define H713_INCAP_ICSC_BYPASS	BIT(31)
+
+/*
  * The capture ring. Three Y slots and three C slots, fixed by the firmware;
  * the slot pitch is 0x1ff000 and C slot i is Y slot i plus 0x5fd000. Y and C
  * always name the same index in every dump taken so far. Measured on the
@@ -257,6 +268,21 @@
 #define H713_SOURCE_VIDEODEC	1
 
 /*
+ * THal_Vp_SetVideoRange: 0 follows the signal, 2 forces full range, anything
+ * else forces limited range. The value matters less than the change: a call
+ * with a new value makes the firmware re-evaluate its picture path for the
+ * VideoDec record AND for the HDMI signal, in that order, and the HDMI one is
+ * the one that puts the input converter back to the signal's colour space.
+ */
+#define H713_VIDEO_RANGE_AUTO	0
+#define H713_VIDEO_RANGE_FULL	2
+
+/* how long the firmware may take to switch the capture off after a record */
+#define H713_REBUILD_TIMEOUT_MS	1500
+/* and how long SetVideoRange may take to reach the converter register */
+#define H713_ICSC_TIMEOUT_MS	500
+
+/*
  * How long the capture gets to come back after the source has been switched
  * away and back. Measured on 07.09.2026 over three cycles: 539 ms, 536 ms and
  * 544 ms from the return of the second RPC until INCAP +0x928 bit 31 was set
@@ -731,6 +757,7 @@
 	phys_addr_t ring_size;
 
 	u32 id_setsource;
+	u32 id_setvideorange;
 
 	struct v4l2_dv_timings timings;
 	u32 plane_size;			/* bytes per plane of the current format */
@@ -1589,6 +1616,90 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static bool h713_hdmirx_icsc_bypassed(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	unsigned int val;
+
+	if (regmap_read(rx->incap, H713_INCAP_ICSC, &val))
+		return false;
+
+	return val & H713_INCAP_ICSC_BYPASS;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Put the capture's input converter back to the HDMI signal's colour space.
+ *
+ * Why it is ever wrong: the VidDec record this driver's display side publishes
+ * says yuv420_888, because that is the only value that keeps the AFBD reader
+ * on NV16. The firmware builds its picture-path filter from the record that
+ * caused the last signal event, and for a YUV source it switches the capture's
+ * input converter to bypass -- correct for a decoder frame, which is already
+ * YCbCr, wrong for this capture, which is fed RGB by the HDMI receiver. From
+ * then on RGB goes into the NV16 ring unconverted and the wall is green. The
+ * first publication does not show it only because it ends in a source switch,
+ * which makes the firmware re-lock the HDMI signal and evaluate that instead.
+ * Measured on the ring: grey with the converter bypassed reads Cb ~ Cr ~ Y
+ * (40/43/40), with it on Cb ~ Cr ~ 128 (doku/nachtlog/S11; the rule and the
+ * register from the firmware, doku/nachtlog/S9).
+ *
+ * How it is put right without a source switch: THal_Vp_SetVideoRange with a
+ * CHANGED value. Same value, nothing happens. A new value makes the firmware
+ * evaluate the picture path twice, first for the VideoDec record (bypass) and
+ * then for the HDMI signal it still has cached (RGB, BT.709) -- and the last
+ * one writes the register. Full range and back to auto is two such changes
+ * and leaves the range setting where the init sequence put it. Nothing else
+ * moves: no window rebuild, no capture enable, no state machine. Measured at
+ * 1080p and 720p, scaler and strides untouched, ring back to YCbCr.
+ *
+ * Called with the capture switched OFF, so no green frame ever reaches the
+ * ring; the wall holds the previous picture until the capture is enabled
+ * after this. Also called as a check where the state should already be right.
+ */
+static void h713_hdmirx_restore_icsc(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, const char *wann)
+{
+	ktime_t start;
+	u32 arg;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!h713_hdmirx_icsc_bypassed(rx))
+		return;
+
+	if (!rx->id_setvideorange) {
+		dev_warn_once(rx->dev,
+			      "Farbwandler: steht auf Bypass (%s) und SetVideoRange fehlt -- das Bild bleibt gruen\n",
+			      wann);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	arg = H713_VIDEO_RANGE_FULL;
+	ret = cpu_comm_call(rx->id_setvideorange, &arg, 1, NULL, 0, 0);
+	if (!ret) {
+		arg = H713_VIDEO_RANGE_AUTO;
+		ret = cpu_comm_call(rx->id_setvideorange, &arg, 1, NULL, 0, 0);
+	}
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(rx->dev,
+			 "Farbwandler: SetVideoRange fehlgeschlagen: %d (%s) -- das Bild ist gruen\n",
+			 ret, wann);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	start = ktime_get();
+	do {
+		if (!h713_hdmirx_icsc_bypassed(rx)) {
+			dev_info(rx->dev,
+				 "Farbwandler: wieder BT.709 (%s), nach %lld ms\n",
+				 wann, ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start));
+			return;
+		}
+		usleep_range(2000, 4000);
+	} while (ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start) < H713_ICSC_TIMEOUT_MS);
+
+	dev_warn(rx->dev,
+		 "Farbwandler: bleibt nach SetVideoRange auf Bypass (%s) -- das Bild ist gruen\n",
+		 wann);
+}
+
 static void h713_hdmirx_rearm(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
 	bool erste = !rx->desc_seen;
@@ -1634,9 +1745,36 @@
 		 */
